
{"id":"69b5c551-86e3-49c0-943a-ec661cb4daed","object":"chat.completion","created":1785015549,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case.get(\\\"lines\\\", [])\\n    trajectory = []\\n    state = \\\"start\\\"\\n    error_line = None\\n    for idx, line in enumerate(lines):\\n        line_number = idx + 1\\n        parts = line.split(\\\": \\\", 1)\\n        if len(parts) != 2:\\n            error_line = line_number\\n            break\\n        prefix, content = parts\\n        if prefix == \\\"Thought\\\":\\n            rec_type = \\\"thought\\\"\\n        elif prefix == \\\"Action\\\":\\n            rec_type = \\\"action\\\"\\n        elif prefix == \\\"Observation\\\":\\n            rec_type = \\\"observation\\\"\\n        elif prefix == \\\"Final\\\":\\n            rec_type = \\\"final\\\"\\n        else:\\n            error_line = line_number\\n            break\\n        if state == \\\"start\\\":\\n            allowed = {\\\"thought\\\", \\\"final\\\"}\\n        elif state == \\\"thought\\\":\\n            allowed = {\\\"action\\\"}\\n        elif state == \\\"action\\\":\\n            allowed = {\\\"observation\\\"}\\n        elif state == \\\"observation\\\":\\n            allowed = {\\\"thought\\\", \\\"final\\\"}\\n        elif state == \\\"final\\\":\\n            allowed = set()\\n        else:\\n            allowed = set()\\n        if rec_type not in allowed:\\n            error_line = line_number\\n            break\\n        if rec_type == \\\"final\\\" and idx != len(lines) - 1:\\n            error_line = line_number\\n            break\\n        trajectory.append({\\\"type\\\": rec_type, \\\"content\\\": content})\\n        state = rec_type\\n    if error_line is not None:\\n        valid =","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for task AGENT-RA-01. The task is to parse and validate interleaved Thought, Action, Action Input, and Observation records. The input `case` is a payload object with a `lines` list and `_case_nonce`. We need to validate the sequence according to rules: for each nonterminal cycle, require Thought -> Action -> Observation. Final only at the end. On first violation, return error_line (1-based index of offending line) and trajectory (valid prefix up to that point, but note: \"valid prefix\" likely means the trajectory records up to the line before the violation? Need to interpret.\n\nFrom the description: \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" The output schema has `error_line` (null or integer) and `trajectory` (array of objects with content and type). If valid, `error_line` should be null, `trajectory` should be all parsed records, `valid` = true. If invalid, `error_line` is the 1-based line number of the first violation, `trajectory` is the valid prefix (presumably all parsed records up to but not including the violation? Or including the violation? The phrase \"valid prefix\" suggests the trajectory of valid records before the violation, so not including the violating line. But need to see examples? We don't have expected outputs, only input fixtures. However we can infer from typical ReAct parsing tasks.\n\nAlso need to handle Action Input? The lines might contain \"Action Input: ...\"? The given fixtures only have lines like \"Thought: ...\", \"Action: ...\", \"Observation: ...\", \"Final: ...\". So we should parse lines that start with those prefixes. Action Input is not present in fixtures, but the candidate artifact mentions \"Action Input\" as a field. Probably we need to treat lines that start with \"Action Input:\" as part of an action? But the task says \"Parse and validate interleaved Thought, Action, Action Input, and Observation records\". However the given payload lines do not include Action Input. But we must implement generically: parse lines with known prefixes.\n\nWe need to decide on the exact parsing: Each line should be parsed into a record. The allowed types: \"thought\", \"action\", \"action_input\", \"observation\", \"final\". But the output schema for trajectory items has only \"content\" (string) and \"type\" (string). So we need to produce those.\n\nThe validation rules:\n- Sequence must be: Thought, then Action, then Observation for each nonterminal cycle. Possibly Action Input can appear after Action? The typical ReAct pattern: Thought -> Action -> Action Input -> Observation. But the candidate artifact mentions \"Action Input\" separately. Since the fixtures don't include it, we might need to allow it optionally after Action. However the description says \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" It doesn't mention Action Input. Possibly Action Input is part of the Action line? Or a separate line that must follow Action? We need to interpret carefully.\n\nGiven the candidate artifact: \"Stay inside this mechanism boundary: ReAct trajectory representation and action validation; reasoning text quality and live tools are excluded. Validate every required field before computing a result. Parse only complete Thought, Action, Observation, or Final records.\" So it mentions only Thought, Action, Observation, Final. But later: \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: valid_turn_order, tool_schema, observation_binding, stop_semantics.\" So the turn order is Thought->Action->Observation.\n\nAlso \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" So Final can appear only as the last record, and it should be a reasoning boundary (i.e., no more cycles after).\n\nThus I'll implement: parse each line, identify the type based on prefix. If line starts with \"Thought:\" -> type \"thought\", content is rest. Similarly \"Action:\" -> \"action\", \"Action Input:\" -> \"action_input\", \"Observation:\" -> \"observation\", \"Final:\" -> \"final\". (Case-insensitive? Probably case-sensitive as given in examples: \"Thought:\", \"Action:\", etc. But we'll do exact match on the prefix string including colon and space? The fixtures have \"Thought: inspect\" so prefix \"Thought: \". We'll strip after prefix. We'll handle lines that don't match any prefix? Possibly ignore or treat as invalid? The task says parse only complete records, so lines that don't match should be considered invalid? Or maybe they are errors. We'll include them as unrecognized? But the output type must be one of the allowed types. Since not specified, I'll assume that any line must match one of the known prefixes, otherwise it's a violation. However the fixtures are clean.\n\nNow validation logic:\n- We iterate through lines.\n- We maintain a state: expected next type? Or simply validate the sequence as we go.\n- The allowed sequence (nonterminal cycles): Thought -> Action -> Observation. But Action Input might be inserted after Action? The typical pattern: Thought -> Action -> Action Input -> Observation. But the problem statement says \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" So it doesn't require Action Input. Maybe Action Input is optional and can only appear after Action and before Observation? But if it appears, does it break the Thought->Action->Observation? The order would be Thought:1, Action:2, Action Input:3, Observation:4. That's fine if we consider Action Input as part of the action step. The valid turn order might allow Action Input after Action. Since not explicitly forbidden, I'll allow Action Input after Action and before Observation. Also, can there be multiple Action Input? Probably not. I'll treat Action Input as a separate record that must follow an action and precede an observation. But the problem says \"Parse only complete Thought, Action, Observation, or Final records.\" It doesn't list Action Input as a record type in that sentence. So maybe Action Input is not a separate record type; rather it's part of the Action content? That could be: \"Action: tool_name\\nAction Input: input\". But in the ReAct format, Action Input is sometimes on the same line as Action? In the given fixtures, only \"Action: lookup\". So no Action Input. To be safe, I'll ignore Action Input for now since it's not in fixtures and the problem says \"Parse only complete Thought, Action, Observation, or Final records.\" So I'll only consider those four types. The mention of \"Action Input\" in the candidate artifact might be a leftover from a broader spec but not required for this task. The output schema doesn't have a specific field for Action Input. So I'll not parse Action Input.\n\nThus the types: thought, action, observation, final.\n\nNow the turn order:\n- Can start with Thought or Final? Probably can start with Thought. Final only at end. So sequence: Thought (cycle start), then Action, then Observation, then repeat. After an Observation, next must be Thought or possibly Final? According to \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" So after Observation, you can have another Thought (start new cycle) or Final (end). So the pattern is: (Thought Action Observation)* optionally Final at the end. Also can there be multiple Thoughts? If there is a Thought after an Observation, that's a new cycle. Could there be multiple thoughts in a row? No, because after Thought must be Action. So sequence is strictly alternating.\n\nBut what about Final? It must be the last line. It doesn't have a content after \"Final:\"? The fixtures show \"Final: done\". So content is \"done\". Final doesn't need subsequent observation.\n\nSo validation algorithm:\n- Initialize empty trajectory list.\n- state = 'start' (expect Thought or Final)\n- For each line (1-indexed):\n   - Parse line: if starts with \"Thought:\" -> type='thought', content=rest\n   - elif starts with \"Action:\" -> type='action', content=rest\n   - elif starts with \"Observation:\" -> type='observation', content=rest\n   - elif starts with \"Final:\" -> type='final', content=rest\n   - else: violation? I'll treat as invalid line, set error_line = current line number, trajectory is what we have so far (valid prefix), valid=False, return.\n   - Check if this record type is allowed in current state:\n      - from 'start' allowed: thought, final. If not, violation.\n      - from 'thought' (after a thought) allowed: action. (since thought must be followed by action)\n      - from 'action' allowed: observation (since action must be followed by observation? But also could be Final? Possibly not, because after action you need observation to complete the cycle. The problem says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" That suggests Final can only appear after an observation or at start? The typical ReAct: after observation you can either continue with thought or final answer. So Final cannot follow Action directly. So after action, only observation is allowed. Also, could Final follow Thought? No, because if you have a thought, you must perform an action. So I'll say after action, only observation allowed.\n      - from 'observation' allowed: thought, final.\n      - from 'final' no further records allowed.\n   - If type is 'final', it must be the last line (i.e., no more lines after). But we can check after parsing all lines, we can also check if we are at the last line. For violation, we can detect if there are more lines after final.\n   - If violation: set error_line, trajectory = current trajectory (before adding this record), valid=False, return.\n   - Else: add record to trajectory, update state.\n- After processing all lines: check if state is a valid terminating state: allowed ending states are observation or final? Actually after a thought, you need action and observation. So state 'thought' is invalid because cycle incomplete. State 'action' is invalid. State 'observation' is valid if final is not required but the sequence can end after an observation? The problem says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" It doesn't say the sequence must end with Final. So ending with Observation is okay? Possibly yes, but typically ReAct ends with Final. However the fixtures all end with \"Final: done\". So we might need to enforce that the last record must be Final? The problem says \"Permit Final only at the end\" meaning if Final appears, it must be at the end. But it doesn't say the sequence must end with Final. However the requirement \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle\" suggests that each cycle must be complete. Ending after an observation is complete? The cycle is complete after observation. So ending after observation is okay. But does the problem allow that? It says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" If the sequence ends with observation, that's a reasoning boundary? It's ambiguous. To be safe, I'll not require Final at end. I'll allow ending with observation, but check that state is 'observation' or 'final'? Also state 'start' if empty? That's fine.\n\nBut we also need to handle the case where a line is \"Final:\" not at the end: that's a violation. Also if there are multiple Finals: violation.\n\nNow, \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So we return error_line as the line number of the offending line (1-indexed). The trajectory should be the records parsed up to the line before the violation. So if violation occurs at line i, trajectory should contain records from lines 1..i-1. If the violation is due to an invalid state (e.g., after thought we get another thought), then the offending line is that line, and trajectory is records before it.\n\nIf no violation, error_line = null, trajectory = all records, valid = true.\n\nEdge case: what if the first line is invalid? Then trajectory should be empty array, error_line = 1.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure that the content string is the rest of the line after the prefix (including leading space? We'll strip leading whitespace from the content? The examples: \"Thought: inspect\" -> content \"inspect\". If we strip, that's fine. We'll strip the prefix and then strip leading whitespace? But the content might have spaces; we should keep as is after prefix and leading space. Typically we take the part after \": \" (colon space). But to be robust, we can find the colon index and take substring after colon+1, then strip? But the prefix might have space after colon? In the fixtures, it's \"Thought: \" (colon space). So we can split on \": \" (colon space) once. That yields prefix and rest. If the line doesn't contain \": \" or the prefix doesn't match, we treat as invalid.\n\nThus parsing: line.split(\": \", 1) -> if len == 2 and first part is one of the allowed types. But \"Final:\" would also be \"Final\" after split? Actually \"Final: done\" splits into [\"Final\", \"done\"]. That's fine. \"Action Input:\" would split into [\"Action Input\", ...]. But we are ignoring that. So we'll only accept \"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\" as prefix types. Case-sensitive? The fixtures use capital first letter. I'll assume exactly as given.\n\nNow implement solve(case):\n- case is a dict with \"lines\" (list of strings) and possibly \"_case_nonce\", but we ignore nonce.\n- Extract lines.\n- Initialize trajectory = [], state = 'start', error_line = None, valid = None.\n- Iterate over lines with index i (0-based), line number = i+1.\n- Parse: \n  - parts = line.split(\": \", 1)\n  - if len(parts) != 2: invalid line -> error_line = i+1, break.\n  - prefix, content = parts\n  - if prefix == \"Thought\": type = \"thought\"\n  - elif prefix == \"Action\": type = \"action\"\n  - elif prefix == \"Observation\": type = \"observation\"\n  - elif prefix == \"Final\": type = \"final\"\n  - else: invalid line -> error_line = i+1, break.\n- Check allowed:\n  - if state == 'start':\n       allowed = {\"thought\", \"final\"}\n  - elif state == 'thought':\n       allowed = {\"action\"}\n  - elif state == 'action':\n       allowed = {\"observation\"}  # because action must be followed by observation\n  - elif state == 'observation':\n       allowed = {\"thought\", \"final\"}\n  - elif state == 'final':\n       allowed = set()  # no more records allowed\n  - else: (unreachable)\n  - if type not in allowed: error_line = i+1, break.\n- If type == 'final':\n  - Check if it's the last line: if i != len(lines)-1: error_line = i+1, break. (Because final must be at end)\n- If no violation, add record to trajectory: {\"type\": type, \"content\": content} (content is the rest after the colon and space, not stripped? We'll keep as is, but typically leading space? Since we split on \": \", the content will start with the next character, which is not whitespace? Actually in \"Thought: inspect\", split gives content=\"inspect\" (no leading space). So fine.)\n- Update state = type.\n- After loop:\n  - if error_line is not None:\n        valid = False\n        # trajectory is already the valid prefix (records before the violating line)\n  - else:\n        # check if final state is acceptable: after processing all lines, state should not be 'thought' or 'action' (incomplete cycle). Also state should not be 'start' if lines non-empty (but if lines empty? we'll allow empty trajectory? But case might have empty lines array? Not in fixtures, but we handle: if lines empty, valid=True, trajectory=[], error_line=null. That seems fine.\n        # So check if state in {'observation', 'final'} or if lines empty. Also state='start' with lines empty is allowed.\n        # If state in {'thought','action'}: then the sequence is incomplete, but the problem says \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" So an incomplete cycle should be a violation. But where is the violation? The last line is the last record, which is a thought or action. That line itself is not necessarily invalid in isolation, but the sequence as a whole is invalid because it doesn't complete. Should we set error_line to the line of the last record? Or maybe no error_line because the last line is valid but the overall structure is invalid? The output requires error_line to be null if valid, integer if invalid. So we need to indicate a violation. The \"first violation\" would be the point where the sequence becomes invalid. If we end with an incomplete cycle, the violation is at the end. The last line (the thought or action) is the start of an incomplete cycle. That line is not necessarily a violation at the moment it was added, but after processing all lines we see it's incomplete. Should we set error_line to the line number of that last line? I think yes, because the sequence is invalid due to missing subsequent records. The \"first violation\" could be interpreted as the first line that cannot be part of a valid complete sequence. In this case, when we add the thought, it's okay because we expect later action and observation. But if there is no later line, then the thought itself becomes a violation because it's not completed. So we can treat it as a violation at that line after the loop. So we set error_line = len(lines) (the last line), trajectory = valid prefix before that line? That would be all lines except the last? But the valid prefix should be records before the violation. So trajectory would be the records before the last line. That seems correct.\n        # However, we need to reconsider: The algorithm currently would have added the thought line to trajectory, and then after loop we discover state is 'thought'. So we need to remove the last record from trajectory and set error_line to that line number. Because the trajectory should be the valid prefix that is complete. So we should not add the incomplete record to trajectory. Better to check after each addition? We can check after adding each record if the state is a terminal state? But we don't know if more lines exist. We can only detect incompleteness after processing all lines. So we should keep a separate list of records, and after the loop, if we detect invalid final state, we set error_line to the last line number (i+1), and the trajectory should be the records before that last line. Since we added the last record, we need to pop it from trajectory. But careful: The last record might be a thought or action. If we pop it, trajectory becomes the valid prefix that ends at previous line. That previous line must be in a valid terminal state? If the previous line was an observation, then the trajectory ends with observation, which is fine. If the previous line was a final, we already would have caught that final not at end. So it's okay.\n        # Alternatively, we can detect incompleteness during the loop by checking when we finish that there are no more lines? That's after the loop. So we'll do post-processing.\n        # Implementation: After loop, if error_line is None, check state. If state in {'thought','action'}:\n            error_line = len(lines)  # last line\n            trajectory.pop()  # remove the last record that caused incomplete\n            valid = False\n        # If state == 'start' and lines non-empty: that would be if first line invalid? But we already handled invalid lines. If first line is valid, state won't be 'start' after processing. So state 'start' only if lines empty.\n        # else valid = True, error_line = None.\n  - Return dict with error_line (None if valid else integer), trajectory, valid.\n\nBut also need to consider that the last line could be a 'final' that is not at end? We already check that when processing final. So final at end is handled.\n\nNow, what about the case where the sequence is valid cycles but ends with observation? That's allowed per our logic. The fixtures all end with Final, but we should not force.\n\nNow, implement in Python.\n\nAlso, handle potential non-string lines? Assume lines are strings.\n\nReturn JSON-compatible dict. Use None for null.\n\nNow, we need to output Python source code in a JSON string with key \"implementation\". The source must define `solve(case)`.\n\nWrite the solve function.\n\nI'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case.get(\"lines\", [])\n    trajectory = []\n    state = \"start\"\n    error_line = None\n    valid = None\n\n    for idx, line in enumerate(lines):\n        line_number = idx + 1\n        # parse\n        parts = line.split(\": \", 1)\n        if len(parts) != 2:\n            error_line = line_number\n            break\n        prefix, content = parts\n        if prefix == \"Thought\":\n            rec_type = \"thought\"\n        elif prefix == \"Action\":\n            rec_type = \"action\"\n        elif prefix == \"Observation\":\n            rec_type = \"observation\"\n        elif prefix == \"Final\":\n            rec_type = \"final\"\n        else:\n            error_line = line_number\n            break\n\n        # check allowed\n        if state == \"start\":\n            allowed = {\"thought\", \"final\"}\n        elif state == \"thought\":\n            allowed = {\"action\"}\n        elif state == \"action\":\n            allowed = {\"observation\"}\n        elif state == \"observation\":\n            allowed = {\"thought\", \"final\"}\n        elif state == \"final\":\n            allowed = set()\n        else:\n            allowed = set()\n        if rec_type not in allowed:\n            error_line = line_number\n            break\n\n        # if final, must be last\n        if rec_type == \"final\" and idx != len(lines) - 1:\n            error_line = line_number\n            break\n\n        # add to trajectory\n        trajectory.append({\"type\": rec_type, \"content\": content})\n        state = rec_type\n\n    if error_line is not None:\n        valid = False\n        # trajectory already is valid prefix (records before violation)\n        # But note that if violation occurred at first line, trajectory is empty, fine.\n    else:\n        # check final state\n        if state in (\"thought\", \"action\"):\n            # incomplete cycle\n            error_line = len(lines)  # last line\n            # remove the last record from trajectory (the incomplete one)\n            trajectory.pop()\n            valid = False\n        elif state == \"start\" and len(lines) > 0:\n            # should not happen, but just in case: no records parsed? Actually if first line invalid, we would have error_line set. So this is for empty lines.\n            valid = True\n            error_line = None\n        else:\n            valid = True\n            error_line = None\n\n    return {\n        \"error_line\": error_line,\n        \"trajectory\": trajectory,\n        \"valid\": valid\n    }\n\nBut we need to consider that after we break due to violation, we have not added the violating record to trajectory, so trajectory is already valid prefix. Good.\n\nEdge case: If the sequence ends with final, state becomes final. Then no further lines. After loop, we check state: final is allowed. valid=True.\n\nIf the sequence ends with observation, state=observation, allowed. valid=True.\n\nIf the sequence ends with thought or action, invalid.\n\nAlso, what about duplicate finals? The check \"if rec_type == \"final\" and idx != len(lines) - 1\" will catch final not at end. If there is a final at the right place but then later another final? The second final would be after first final, state would be final, then allowed set is empty, so second final is not allowed -> error_line set. So that works.\n\nNow, we should also consider that the line might have extra colons? Our split with maxsplit=1 handles that. So content could contain colon.\n\nAlso, what about empty content? e.g., \"Thought:\" with nothing after? split gives [ \"Thought\", \"\" ] -> content is empty string. That's fine.\n\nNow, ensure we don't mutate case.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable object.\n\nNow, format as JSON for \"implementation\" key: The Python source as a string. We'll escape newlines, quotes, backslashes.\n\nWe'll write the code in a clean way.\n\nAlso, note that the output schema requires `error_line` to be integer or null, not string. So we use None for null.\n\nAlso, ensure that `valid` is boolean.\n\nNow, produce the final answer.\n\nBut before finalizing, consider potential corner cases from the fixtures. The fixtures have multiple cases, but we are not given expected outputs. However we can test mentally:\n\nCase fg1-agent-ra-01-b-000: lines:\n- Thought: inspect\n- Action: lookup\n- Observation: found\n- Thought: answer\n- Final: done\n\nProcess:\nline1: Thought -> state=thought, added.\nline2: Action -> state=action, added.\nline3: Observation -> state=observation, added.\nline4: Thought -> allowed from observation? yes, state=thought, added.\nline5: Final -> allowed from thought? No, state=thought allows only action. So error_line = 5, trajectory = records before line5: [Thought1,Action,Obs,Thought2]. So valid=False. That seems plausible. The expected behavior might be that after Thought you need Action, but here Final is after Thought, which violates. So that case is invalid. good.\n\nCase fg1-agent-ra-01-b-001: lines:\n- Thought: inspect\n- Action: lookup\n- Thought: invalid order\n- Thought: answer\n- Final: done\n\nProcess:\nline1: Thought -> state=thought\nline2: Action -> state=action\nline3: Thought -> after action, allowed is observation, so error_line=3, trajectory = [Thought1, Action]. So invalid. That seems right.\n\nCase b-002 same as b-001.\n\nSo our logic seems consistent.\n\nNow, the problem statement says \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" For b-000, first violation at line5, valid prefix is first four lines. For b-001, violation at line3, valid prefix is first two lines.\n\nNow, what about a sequence that ends with incomplete cycle, e.g., only Thought? lines=[\"Thought: hello\"]:\nline1: Thought added, state=thought. After loop, state=thought,